WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
19 August 2025, 20:00 UTC. A flat map of the whole Earth on a dark background: sulfate glows green, dust lilac, smoke red-orange, sea salt grey-blue. A white line scans it from west to east, starting at 180 degrees in the Pacific, and the sound moves from left to right with it. Each band of latitude has its own note, higher further north. You hear the smooth hum of sulfate over the North Pacific, including a thin streak drifting west of Hawaii from Kilauea.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
The line crosses the open eastern Pacific. Little stands out above the usual haze, and the sound almost stops: only a soft wind and a tick every 30 degrees of longitude.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
A glass chime marks the first plume found in the image: sea salt churned up by storms in the Southern Ocean, heard as low surf. Further north the line reaches North America, where green sulfate hums over the cities of the east.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Off the United States east coast, the white spiral of Hurricane Erin: a brief surf sound on a middle note, at about 27 degrees north.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.000
The Saharan dust plume. Two chimes mark where it begins, over the Caribbean and the open Atlantic. Dust sounds as sand grains on the note for 20 degrees north, and the grains thicken into the loudest sound of the scan as the lilac band widens towards Africa.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.000
A deep tick at Greenwich. Over Spain, fires flash red-orange: a crackle on a high note, at about 40 degrees north, above the dust.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
A chime and a low crackle: smoke from seasonal burning in southern Africa, a red-orange cloud over Angola and the Congo spreading out over the Atlantic. Over Italy a sulfate hum rises where Mount Etna's plume crosses southern Europe, while the dust grains continue across the Sahara.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
The dust thins over Arabia and the Middle East, and green sulfate spreads in from the Persian Gulf.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Two chimes: sulfate from pollution over India and South Asia, at about 20 degrees north, then over the Indian Ocean and Indonesia, just south of the equator. The hum becomes the strongest layer. A few grains return on higher notes, from dust over the deserts of Central Asia.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.000
East Asia and Australia. Sulfate hums over China and Japan, and grey-blue sea salt swirls in the Southern Ocean below Australia sound as surf.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.000
The western Pacific: bright green sulfate streams east of Australia and over the North Pacific. The line reaches 180 degrees, back where it started, and the scan ends.
